{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries''' ({{Nihongo|農林水産省|Nōrin-suisan-shō}}, commonly designated by the acronym '''MAFF''' or colloquially as '''Nōsui-shō''') is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for national agricultural production, food security strategy, forestry resource stewardship, commercial marine fisheries regulation, and food quality safety standards. The primary sector overseen by the ministry represents approximately 4.4 percent of national Gross Domestic Product (173 billion Sen) and occupies roughly 13 percent of national land area for intensive cultivation, with an additional 70 percent of mountainous terrain managed as commercial and watershed forest reserves.

The ministry commands an annual state budget of approximately 40 billion Sen, representing roughly 2.5 percent of total government expenditure. Subordinate statutory bodies include the [[Fisheries Agency (Okaiken)|Fisheries Agency]], the [[Forestry Agency (Okaiken)|Forestry Agency]], and the Food Safety Commission. The incumbent Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries is [[Daisuke Kondo]] of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), who assumed office on 20 May 2026 within the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. In coalition governance, Minister Kondo has led a major strategic pivot away from market-driven agricultural consolidation toward statutory food self-sufficiency targets, guaranteed baseline producer price floors, and direct income subsidies for smallholders in depopulating rural provinces.

==Historical development and agrarian policy==

===Imperial origins and the post-war land reform===

The ministry traces its institutional heritage to the Ministry of Agriculture and Commerce (農商省, ''Nōshō-shō''), founded in 1881 to introduce scientific agronomy and expand export silk cultivation. In 1897, the department was reconstituted as the Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry (農林省, ''Nōrin-shō''), prioritizing domestic rice production to sustain rapid urbanization and establishing the foundation for the nationwide agricultural cooperative network.

Following the end of the second world conflict, the ministry executed the revolutionary Land Reform Act of 1947 under democratic reconstruction authorities. The statute dismantled landlord tenant estates, transferring approximately 1.5 million hectares of arable soil directly to tenant farmers and creating an egalitarian class of smallholder owner-cultivators. The subsequent Agricultural Cooperative Act of 1948 established the statutory framework for the [[OkaikenCOOP]] federation, which organized collective fertilizer purchasing, cold-storage warehousing, and crop marketing. Through the Food Staple Control Act of 1951, the ministry stabilized the national diet by guaranteeing price supports and state rice purchases.

===Modern challenges and the food sovereignty doctrine===

The 2001 Central Government Reform reorganized MAFF's internal bureaus, creating the Food Safety Commission and reinforcing the autonomous operational remits of the Fisheries Agency and Forestry Agency. Over recent decades, the ministry has confronted acute demographic contraction across rural prefectures, with the average age of Sanese farmers rising past sixty-eight years.

The vulnerability of the national food system was exposed during the En currency crisis of early 2026, which spiked the cost of imported animal feed, chemical fertilizers, and wheat by over twenty percent, and the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 2026, which temporarily disabled algorithmic warehouse logistics across agricultural distributors. Under Minister Daisuke Kondo, the ministry enacted the Food Sovereignty Protection Plan, establishing strategic national grain reserves, guaranteeing producer prices for domestic wheat and soybeans, and barring automated systems from unilateral allocation of agricultural credit.

